// `--no-heap` proves the PROGRAM, and a program is what `main` reaches. The positive half of
// tests/xfail/noheap_flag_reached_dispatch.d, and the regression it pins is a real one that shipped for
// exactly one commit: merely NAMING `std::collections::SlotMap` in an import failed a `--no-heap` build.
//
// Why that happened, because the shape recurs: `Handle` is non-generic, so its hand-written `deserialize`
// body is always emitted — a generic container's is gated behind `when [V: Deserializable<V>]` and pruned
// when unused, which is why no other collection had ever exposed this. That body dispatches through the
// `Deserializer` contract, and an indirect dispatch is unprovable by construction (the compiler cannot see
// which implementation runs). The flag was rejecting it where it was EMITTED rather than where it was
// REACHED, which made `--no-heap` mean "no body anywhere in the import closure may dispatch" — a far
// stronger claim than the flag advertises, and one no stdlib could satisfy.
//
// The direct-allocation half of the same checker always worked this way (see the sibling xfail, whose
// diagnostic is a chain from `main`), so this is the two halves agreeing rather than a new rule.
//
// ⚠️ The import is load-bearing and must not be "tidied away" as unused: naming the symbol is the whole
// test. `SlotMap` is never constructed here, and it allocates, so constructing one would fail the build
// for an entirely different and correct reason.
import { std::collections::SlotMap, std::collections::Handle };

fn int32 main() {
    // A handle built and read with no map in sight: `Handle` is a plain value, so none of this allocates
    // and none of it reaches the serde body that dispatches.
    Handle h = Handle.of(index: 2, generation: 3ui32);
    InlineArray<int32>#(4) xs = [0; 4];
    xs[cast<isize>(h.generation)] = cast<int32>(h.index) + 23;
    return xs[3];
}
